# Defence in depth

### Escaping, sanitising, CSP and containment in rung 6

Tutors Ladder, rung 6 (Guarded), talk 2

---

## Three walls, one attack

```
course Markdown ──► escaping ──► sanitising ──► page ──► CSP in the browser
                      (rung 2)     (rung 6)               (rung 6)
course folder  ──► containedPath in every adapter ──► gen
                      (rung 6)
```

Each wall assumes the one before it can fail. A bug in one is a finding, not a breach.

---

## Escaping is not sanitising

**Escaping** changes how text is *written* so it stays text: `<` becomes `&lt;`.
**Sanitising** decides whether a value is *allowed at all*.

Rung 3 escapes everything. This still works:

```html
<a href="javascript:alert(document.domain)">the reading list</a>
```

No character in it needs escaping. The browser runs it when a reader clicks.
`packages/lite/src/markdown.ts` in rung 3 even said so: "sanitising links such as `javascript:` is rung 6".

---

## Allow-list, never block-list

`packages/lite/src/url.ts`:

```ts
const ALLOWED: Record<UrlUse, ReadonlySet<string>> = {
  link: new Set(["http", "https", "mailto"]),
  image: new Set(["http", "https"]),
};

export function safeUrl(url: string, use: UrlUse): string | null {
  const read = url.replace(/[\u0000- \u007f-\u009f]/g, "").toLowerCase();
  const scheme = read.match(/^([a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*):/)?.[1];
  if (scheme !== undefined) return ALLOWED[use].has(scheme) ? url : null;
  const colon = read.indexOf(":");
  const pathStart = read.search(/[/?#]/);
  if (colon !== -1 && (pathStart === -1 || colon < pathStart)) return null;
  return url;
}
```

A block-list of `javascript:` misses `JaVaScRiPt:`, `java\tscript:` and next year's trick.

---

## Read the URL the way a browser does

| Payload | Why a naive filter misses it |
| --- | --- |
| `JaVaScRiPt:alert(1)` | schemes are case-insensitive |
| `java\tscript:alert(1)` | browsers delete tabs and newlines inside a URL |
| `\u0001javascript:alert(1)` | and strip leading control characters |
| `&#106;avascript:alert(1)` | an entity; escaping makes it harmless text, the allow-list refuses it anyway |
| `data:image/svg+xml;base64,...` | an SVG can carry an `onload` |

A refused address is shown as its text in a `<span class="blocked">`, so the page still reads.

---

## Testing a sanitiser: an XSS corpus

`packages/lite/tests/xss-corpus.test.ts` has two halves:

- **table-driven**: 15 hostile URLs and 11 hostile texts, each with the reason it is dangerous, through
  every Markdown construct and every field of every kind of learning object
- **property-based**: 3,000 URLs from a seeded generator mixing schemes, case, control characters,
  entities and encoded colons. fast-check is rung 5's, so this rung grows its own (mulberry32)

The oracle is the browser's algorithm, not lite's: Node's WHATWG `new URL(...)` decides the real scheme.

---

## A Teaching Failure from the corpus

Take `safeUrl` out of `markdown.ts` and the gate says:

```
XSS corpus: "javascript:alert(1)" (the classic: runs on click) reached the page
as href="javascript:alert(1" opens as javascript:.
Course text must be escaped (html`` in packages/lite/src/html.ts) and every course
URL must pass safeUrl (packages/lite/src/url.ts) before it reaches an href or src.
```

The payload, why it matters, what reached the page and the file to fix (L4).

---

## Wall three: Content Security Policy

`packages/lite/src/html.ts`:

```ts
export const CSP = `default-src 'none'; img-src 'self' https:; ` +
  `style-src '${STYLE_HASH}'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'`;
```

- `default-src 'none'` and no `script-src`: **no script runs at all**, inline or loaded
- the one inline `<style>` is allowed by its SHA-256 hash, never `'unsafe-inline'`
- `base-uri 'none'` stops an injected `<base>` re-pointing every relative link

A meta tag cannot set `frame-ancestors`; that needs a response header from the host.

---

## The CSP at work in Chromium

Inject a script and a `javascript:` link into a rung 6 page by hand and open it:

```
Executing inline script violates the following Content Security Policy
directive 'default-src 'none''. ...
Running the JavaScript URL violates the following Content Security Policy
directive 'default-src 'none''. ...
```

`packages/lite/tests/csp.test.ts` keeps it true: the hash matches the style, the policy is the first meta
after the charset on every page, and it has no `unsafe-*`. Change the style and the test prints the new hash.

---

## Upstream and CSP

- `tests/security/header-contract.json` requires a `Content-Security-Policy` header ("the main defence if
  course markdown ever renders unsafe HTML")
- `tests/security/known-response-gaps.txt` records that no app sends one yet: the reader loads Iconify, KaTeX,
  Mermaid, PDF and video embeds, and Supabase realtime, so a policy needs an inventory first
- A static site with no script can afford the strict policy today. The reader will need a report-only rollout.

---

## Wall four: containment at the port

`packages/gen/src/file-system-port.ts`:

```ts
export function containedPath(path: string): string {
  if (path.includes("\0")) throw new PathEscapeError(path, "it holds a NUL byte");
  const slashed = path.replaceAll("\\", "/");
  if (slashed.startsWith("/") || /^[a-zA-Z]:/.test(slashed)) throw new PathEscapeError(path, "it is absolute");
  ...
  if (parts.includes("..")) throw new PathEscapeError(path, 'it climbs out with ".."');
  return parts.join("/");
}
```

`NodeFileSystem` also resolves the real path, so a symbolic link out of the course is refused:
`refused to read "course.md": a link leads outside the course folder.`

---

## Why at the adapter, and an Honest Double

- The adapter is where a path meets real files, so the check cannot be skipped by a new caller
- An architecture rule: every class that `implements FileSystemPort` calls `containedPath`
- `MemoryFileSystem` can hold links too, so the same contract cases run on both adapters:

```ts
await assert.rejects(fs.readText("topic-1/leak.md"), refused);   // a link out
assert.equal(await fs.readText("inside.md"), "# Topic ✓\n");    // a link that stays in
```

If the double were laxer than the disk, gen's fast tests would lie about containment.

---

## And for links on a page: V021

```
warning: V021 topic-01-attack/unit-1-payloads/note-1-links/note.md: This page uses
"../../../answers/solutions.md", which is outside its own folder, so the link breaks
when this is copied into another course. Move the file into this folder and link to it there.
```

The message is upstream's `messages.reachesOutside`, word for word. A warning, not an error: the author
decides. Course, unit and side pages may link anywhere, as upstream's `STAYS`.

---

## Summary

| Wall | Stops | Tested by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Escaping | text becoming markup | the corpus, text half |
| Sanitising | script-bearing URLs | the corpus, URL half and 3,000 generated cases |
| CSP | anything that slips past both | `csp.test.ts`, and a real browser in the lab |
| Containment | reading outside the course | the port contract on both adapters |

Next: who may read, and what we install.

